## Impact
A Denial of Service (DoS) attack can occur when an attacker is able to prevent the contract from functioning correctly, often by causing transactions to fail. In the context of your smart contract, a potential DoS attack could occur in the redeem function. If the payable(recipient).call{value: address(this).balance}("") fails for any reason, the require statement will cause the entire transaction to revert. This means that the tokens will remain locked in the contract, effectively making them unavailable for future transactions.

## Proof of Concept
An attacker could exploit this by causing the payable(recipient).call{value: address(this).balance}("") to fail, for example by creating a contract that always throws an exception when it receives Ether. If this contract is the recipient of the redeem function, it will cause the function to fail, locking up the tokens in the contract and causing a Denial of Service.
```
contract MaliciousContract {
    function () external payable {
        require(false, "This contract always fails");
    }
}
